from research_schema import F

F('calligraphy','Calligraphy as text and form','Calligraphy can transmit words while organizing ornament and the pictorial field. A specimen’s placement, script scale and framing remain observable when its wording is unreadable. This corpus does not turn an untranscribed inscription into a translation or an attribution.','Decorative use does not make writing meaningless; inability to read the slide does not prove the text has no content.','met-calligraphy')
F('zoomorphic-calligraphy','Zoomorphic calligraphy','A documented peacock-shaped composition demonstrates writing arranged into an animal form. It provides a parallel for the lecture’s horse-shaped calligraphy, not a reading of that different text. Follow the silhouette and text placement separately from identifying the words.','An animal outline made from writing is not a naturalistic animal study or a licence to invent its inscription.','met-peacock-script')
F('muraqqa','Muraqqa: the assembled album','An album can gather paintings and calligraphic specimens with borders and mounts. The resulting page may bring together works of different dates and origins. The course’s composite Gulshan page makes this material assembly explicit; its components should not all inherit one maker or date.','An album page is not necessarily a continuously illustrated manuscript narrative or a single artist’s unified painting.','met-mughal-post',aliases='muraqqaʿ|album folio')
F('album-collecting','Collecting portraits into albums','The lecture’s Witsen example shows a portrait framed by European-language annotation and numbering. Mughal album histories provide comparative evidence that assembling and reframing images is an active practice. Original production, later mounting and overseas collecting are distinct historical acts.','A collector’s name does not identify the painter or prove that the portrait was originally commissioned for that collection.','met-mughal-post')
F('manuscript-history','Manuscript illustration and the history of a page','Mughal illustrated manuscripts were produced through courtly workshop practices. Narrative text, painting, later borders and album reuse need separate attention. A modern slide or museum image can show only one stage of a page’s history.','Date of the literary text, date of illustration and date of later mounting need not coincide.','met-mughal-pre')
F('mughal-atelier','The Mughal atelier and multiple makers','Akbar’s court supported a workshop combining Persian, Indian and European resources. The course’s paired versions and studies show different forms of adaptation. Attribution to a named painter is distinct from proving that one person completed all drawing, colouring, inscription and mounting.','A court workshop is not evidence that every sheet is unsigned, anonymous or the work of the same hand.','met-mughal-pre')
F('persianate','Persianate connections and local production','Persianate courtly culture supplied literary, visual and architectural connections across the Deccan and Mughal settings. These connections operated alongside local traditions and makers. In the corpus, the label marks a historically framed comparison rather than a claim that every object was manufactured in Iran.','Persianate and Persian-made are not interchangeable; cultural participation does not erase regional production.','met-deccan met-mughal-pre')
F('firangi-sazi','Firangi-sazi and European-derived pictorial devices','Scholarship describes firangi-sazi as working in a European manner, including perspective, shading and subjects encountered through imported prints. The term names selective adaptation in particular Persianate contexts. It should not be used as proof that every local artist copied one identifiable European original.','European subject, imported print, adapted composition and European manufacturing technique are four different claims.','nature-firangi',aliases='farangi-sazi|European manner')
F('christian-adaptation','Christian imagery in South Asian and Persianate settings','Mughal and Persianate artists encountered and adapted European images, including Christian subjects. They could change setting, figures, handling or purpose. Identifying a Virgin and Child or deposition subject does not independently establish the artist’s or commissioner’s religious allegiance.','Shared subject matter does not prove literal copying, conversion, or the use of the same medium as a European model.','met-mughal-pre nature-firangi')
F('print-transfer','Prints as mobile visual resources','Prints can travel as objects and supply designs for paintings, album pages or new prints. The corpus distinguishes a demonstrable source–adaptation relation from a parallel chosen for comparison. Chronology matters: an object made later cannot have been the direct model for an earlier work.','Japanese-inspired composition need not involve Japanese printing technology; a later comparison is not an earlier source.','met-japonisme')
F('naturalism','Naturalism and selective observation','Jahangir’s patronage included close studies of plants and animals. Naturalistic attention can coexist with an invented setting or imperial allegory. Describe which details appear observed rather than treating the whole image as a factual record.','Recognizable anatomy or modelled volume does not make an allegorical event historically real.','met-mughal-post')
F('perspective','Perspective and systems of pictorial space','The course compares stacked, flattened or multiple-viewpoint spaces with European-derived recession and central perspective. Scholarship on firangi-sazi documents selective adoption of these devices. Overlap, shading and a receding floor are observations; a precise geometric system requires stronger evidence.','Depth is not always single-point perspective, and a non-single-point image is not necessarily an unsuccessful attempt at European geometry.','nature-firangi')
F('chiaroscuro','Chiaroscuro: light and dark modelling','Chiaroscuro concerns the organization of light and dark to establish form and pictorial effect. The National Gallery’s Leonardo discussion provides a specific context. The term describes tonal handling rather than an identifiable chemical pigment or a guarantee of documentary realism.','Chiaroscuro concerns tonal contrast; sfumato concerns softened transitions and contours.','ng-leonardo')
F('sfumato','Sfumato: softened transitions','Sfumato describes softened transitions that avoid sharply bounded outlines, associated with Leonardo’s handling. In the lecture’s comparison, it should be distinguished from the presence of a dark background alone. The cited National Gallery page concerns the London Virgin of the Rocks, not an attribution of the slide’s version.','A dark setting is insufficient to establish sfumato; inspect how edges and tonal transitions are handled.','ng-leonardo')
F('surface-depth','Flat pattern and represented depth','Japonisme encouraged European experiments with contour, cropping and decorative surface. In the course, patterned textiles, flattened colour and modelled bodies can operate within the same work. Describe their relationship instead of forcing every image into an entirely flat or entirely illusionistic category.','A decorative surface does not necessarily eliminate depth, and a figure with shading does not make the whole composition naturalistic.','met-japonisme')
F('japonisme','Japonisme: reception and transformation','Japonisme concerns European responses to Japanese art and objects. Artists adapted viewpoints, contours, cropping and decorative arrangements in different ways. Cassatt’s documented intaglio process and Monet’s oil painting show that influence need not mean importing the source medium unchanged.','Japonisme is not the same as Japanese authorship, Japanese subject matter or Japanese woodblock manufacture.','met-japonisme nga-cassatt mfa-japonaise')
F('kalighat','Kalighat painting and the urban market','Kalighat painters made images for a growing urban and pilgrimage market, often using bold contours and comparatively open grounds. Subjects included deities and social commentary. The economic setting and efficient handling should be considered alongside the finished image’s aesthetic decisions.','Kalighat painting is not automatically a lithographic print; a familiar popular subject does not identify the process.','va-kalighat')
F('social-satire','Social satire and specific visual evidence','Kalighat imagery could address changing social behaviour and public controversies. The Elokeshi–Mahant story provides a documented case, while babu imagery addresses urban conduct and dress. Separate a named narrative from a general interpretation of animals, gestures or domestic conflict.','Not every humorous-looking image is the same scandal, and a moral reading should not become an unsupported account of a historical event.','cleveland-kalighat rom-elokeshi')
F('still-life','Still life: objects, possessions and pictorial choices','Northern European still lifes organize objects into deliberate displays of surfaces, abundance, collection or mortality. Imported goods can connect the domestic image to distant production. Yet the presence of expensive objects alone does not prove the depicted arrangement existed as a real household inventory.','Still life is a pictorial category, not an assurance that every object carries a fixed moral meaning.','met-still-life')
F('pronk','Pronk and sumptuous display','Pronk still life emphasizes sumptuous objects and conspicuous abundance. Reflective vessels, costly foods and imported wares can demonstrate pictorial skill while evoking wealth and circulation. Interpret these effects with the specific arrangement, rather than treating luxury as a complete explanation.','Pronk describes ostentatious display; it is not a synonym for every still life or a specific pigment technique.','met-still-life')
F('vanitas','Vanitas and reminders of transience','Vanitas readings attend to reminders of time, mortality and the instability of possessions. A timepiece or other transience motif supplies a more specific basis than luxury alone. The course’s interpretations should identify the actual object supporting the reading.','A wealthy display is not automatically a vanitas, and one moral theme does not exhaust its visual or economic significance.','met-still-life')
F('portrait-miniature','Portrait miniatures and close viewing','Small portable portraits could serve personal and political relationships, including loyalty or affection. Their intimate scale changes how they are held and viewed. A miniature portrait embedded inside a larger painting is also a represented object within that painting’s own argument.','Miniature is not a licence to call every small manuscript image a European portrait miniature with the same social function.','va-miniature')
F('botanical-illustration','Botanical illustration and maker credit','The Edinburgh account records that Roxburgh commissioned drawings from Indian artists. Scientific authority, collecting, authorship of a botanical text and execution of its images are distinct roles. The corpus therefore retains named commissioners without automatically making them the painters.','A plate associated with Roxburgh does not establish that Roxburgh personally drew or coloured it.','rbge-roxburgh')
F('photographic-mediation','Photographs as mediated evidence','The Beato material documents staged or selected views and hand colouring involving additional makers. A photograph records a particular view while also reflecting posing, framing, market and production choices. A museum installation photograph likewise represents one viewpoint and moment.','Photographic appearance is not proof of unstaged documentary neutrality or access to everything outside the frame.','ludwig-beato getty-beato')
